# The Killers’ Dave Keuning speaks out after surviving a life-threatening stroke

> 🎵 *Curated Music Story from NME Music Official Publications*

### 📖 The Full Story

Music News The Killers’ Dave Keuning speaks out after surviving a life-threatening stroke "By some miracle I am fine" By Tom Skinner | 27th November 2026 Dave Keuning of The Killers. CREDIT: Chris Phelps

The Killers guitarist Dave Keuning has spoken out about suffering a life-threatening stroke recently, saying he had just a 40 per cent chance of survival.

Writing on Instagram yesterday (Thursday November 26), the 50-year-old musician opened up about having a “major stroke” two weeks ago. He was taken “straight to the hospital”, and subsequently underwent surgery.

### 🎤 Key Highlights & Community Reactions

Keuning thanked his wife for “acting fast”, adding: “The first hour after a stroke is critical when it comes to getting to the hospital. I had a blood clot in my brain that cut off the blood supply to the entire right side of my brain.

“By the time I arrived at the hospital, I was blind in one eye and had lost function on my left side, but I still wasn’t able to realise I was having a stroke. The doctors said I had a 40 per cent chance [of] survival and I would have disabilities after. But by some miracle I am fine.”

He continued: “And yes I can play guitar just as good as before. It was the first thing I checked when I woke up from surgery.

“I feel extremely fortunate that I am alive and well, and that I can use my hands and feet and talk just like before. I am thankful for my wife, my son, my daughter, and all of my bandmates, who I feel closer to than ever.”

Keuning went on hiatus from touring with The Killers in 2017 , citing the Las Vegas band’s gruelling live schedule. He returned to the studio to contribute to their 2021 album , ‘Pressure Machine’ , and has since made sporadic on-stage appearances with Brandon Flowers and co.

